3) Contracting / self‑employment income (the $12,500)
- Report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C.
- You’ll also owe self‑employment tax (Schedule SE), currently 15.3% on net self‑employment income (with the usual adjustment).
- You can deduct one‑half of your self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.

4) Home‑office deduction (165 sq ft exclusive; home = 780 sq ft; rent $1,720/mo)
- You meet the exclusive & regular use test for a home office if that 165 sq ft is used only for the business. Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5/sq ft up to 300 sq ft → 165 × $5 = $825 deduction.
  b) Actual expense method: allocate actual home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.) by the office percentage. Your office percentage = 165/780 ≈ 21.15%. Annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640 → rent portion ≈ $4,366. Add your share of utilities, internet, renter’s insurance, etc., times 21.15%. Actual method will likely give a larger deduction (based on rent alone ~$4,366 vs $825), but requires records and you cannot deduct more than the net profit from that business. Also home‑office deduction reduces net Schedule C profit and thus reduces SE tax.
- Recommendation: if the contracting income is reported on Schedule C and you have documentation for rent/utilities, the actual method probably gives a bigger benefit. If you have limited records or want simplicity, use the simplified method.

5) Unreimbursed expenses ($950)
