Thanks — this is enough to give clear, practical guidance. I’ll summarize what you can (likely) deduct, whether to itemize vs. take the standard deduction, how to treat the home office, the forms you’ll need, and next steps/questions so you get everything reported correctly.

Quick headline recommendations
- Likely take the 2023 standard deduction for head of household ($20,800) — you probably won’t beat that with the itemizable amounts you listed.  
- Report the $12,500 IT contracting as self-employment income on Schedule C, pay self‑employment tax (Schedule SE), and deduct business expenses (including home office and the $950 if those expenses relate to the contracting).  
- Home office deduction is allowed for your contracting work (if the space is used exclusively and regularly for the business). You can choose the simplified method (easy) or the regular method (likely larger for your rent amount).  
- Student loan interest is only deductible for interest paid in the tax year and is subject to income phaseouts — given your W‑2 and other income you may not be able to claim it; please clarify the years of payment.  
- Interest from Marcus and E*TRADE is taxable and must be reported; because total interest (~$1,930) > $1,500 you’ll likely attach Schedule B.

What you can deduct (and where)
- Self‑employment business expenses: Deduct on Schedule C. This includes:
  - The $950 unreimbursed expenses if they were for the contracting work.
  - Ordinary & necessary business expenses (equipment, software, supplies, phone portion used for business, contractor fees, etc.). Keep receipts/bank records.
- Home office:
  - Eligibility: must be used exclusively and regularly for your contracting business and be your principal place of business. From your note (165 sq ft exclusive) that sounds OK — keep documentation (floor plan, photos, dates).
  - Two methods:
    - Simplified: $5/sq ft up to 300 sq ft → 165 × $5 = $825 (enter on Schedule C).
    - Regular (actual) method: allocate actual home expenses by business-use percentage. Your business-use % = 165 / 780 ≈ 21.15%. Rent is $1,720 × 12 = $20,640 → rent portion ≈ $4,365. Add your portion of utilities, renters’ insurance, maintenance, etc., for a larger deduction. Use Form 8829 (attach to Schedule C) to calculate the deduction. For renters, you cannot take mortgage depreciation but rent/utilities are deductible proportionally.
  - Which to use: regular method will likely give a larger deduction given your high rent, but it requires more recordkeeping and Form 8829.
- Self‑employment tax deduction: You’ll pay SE tax on net self‑employment income (Schedule SE). One-half of your SE tax is an “above‑the‑line” deduction on Schedule 1 (reduces AGI).
- Student loan interest:
  - Only interest paid in the tax year (2023) is deductible, up to $2,500, and it phases out for single/head-of-household MAGI roughly between $75,000 and $90,000 (2023). With your W‑2 + contracting + interest income your MAGI is likely > $90k, so you may not qualify — and interest paid in earlier years (2016–2020) is not deductible on your 2023 return. Please confirm exact payment years and amounts paid in 2023.
- Charitable contributions:
  - $800 is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). It does not help if you take the standard deduction.
- Interest income:
  - $1,450 + $480 = $1,930 — report this on Form 1040. Because interest > $1,500, attach Schedule B.

Should you itemize or take standard deduction?
- Standard deduction (Head of Household 2023): $20,800.
- From the items you listed, your likely itemized deductions are small (only $800 charitable and maybe some state income tax withheld — but SALT capped at $10k). Unless you have sizable other deductible items (large state/local taxes paid, mortgage interest, high unreimbursed medical expenses, or substantial charitable gifts beyond $20k), the standard deduction will be larger. So I recommend taking the standard deduction almost certainly — unless you have additional itemizable amounts you haven’t listed.

Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $12,500 contracting income and business expenses (including home office).
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax.
- Schedule 1 — for adjustments like half of SE tax and (if allowed) student loan interest.
- If you use the regular home‑office method: Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home).
- Schedule B — to report interest income (since total interest > $1,500).
- 1099s / 1099-NEC: include any 1099-NEC you receive for contracting and 1099-INT for interest (report amounts even if you do not receive forms).
- Arizona state return: AZ Form 140 (Resident Arizona return) — report same income on state return (and follow AZ-specific adjustments).

Other tax items to be aware of
- Estimated tax payments: Because you have self‑employment income, you may owe quarterly estimated taxes (Form 1040‑ES) or face underpayment penalties if you didn’t pay enough during the year.
- Recordkeeping: keep receipts/invoices for business expenses, charity receipts, documentation supporting home office size and exclusive use, 1099s and 1090-INTs, and bank records.
- If you owe, you’ll need to pay federal and state taxes when you file (or in installments). If you had sufficient withholding from the W‑2 it might cover part of the tax liability.

Questions / clarifications I need to finalize a precise recommendation or a rough tax estimate
1. Is the $12,500 gross receipts (before any business expenses)? Did you receive any 1099‑NEC forms for that work?  
2. Do the $950 unreimbursed expenses relate to the contracting work (yes/no)? If yes, what are they (software, travel, supplies, phone, etc.)?  
3. The student loan interest: you wrote “$4,400 (Navient, 2016–2020).” Does that mean those interest payments were paid in 2016–2020 (not in 2023)? Or did you pay $4,400 in 2023? Only 2023 interest payments are potentially deductible.  
4. Do you have other itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes paid, large medical expenses, more charitable donations) not listed here?  
5. Do you want me to: (a) estimate your federal tax bill / refund roughly using these numbers and by choosing simplified vs regular home office, or (b) draft a checklist/email you can send to a tax preparer with all these items?

