Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — useful detail. I can walk through what’s likely deductible, whether to itemize, how to treat the home office, and which forms you’ll need. A few quick clarifying questions before I finalize recommendations (they affect the answers):

- Were the $4,400 in student loan interest payments paid in 2023, or are those totals from 2016–2020 as written? (Only interest paid in 2023 is deductible on a 2023 return.)
- Are the $950 “unreimbursed expenses” for your W-2 job (employee unreimbursed expenses) or for your contracting work (self-employment expenses)?
- Did you receive any 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC / 1099-INT or 1099-DIV for the contracting or interest amounts? (You should report them; I can incorporate exact 1099 amounts if you have them.)

High-level answers (based on the numbers you gave):

1) Standard deduction vs. itemize
- 2023 head-of-household standard deduction: $20,800.
- Likely outcome: You will almost certainly take the standard deduction. Your known potential itemizable amount is small (charitable cash $800). You reported no mortgage interest and likely limited state/local tax or property tax info; without substantial mortgage interest, large medical expenses, or >$20k of SALT and other itemizable deductions, itemizing will almost certainly be less than the $20,800 standard deduction.
- Action: If you have large unrevealed deductions (big property tax or mortgage interest) tell me and I’ll recalc.

2) Charitable contribution
- $800 cash donation with receipt: deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction, you do not get an extra deduction for that $800.

3) Student loan interest
- Only interest you actually paid in 2023 can be deducted (maximum $2,500, phased out by MAGI).
- Based on the W-2 and additional income you provided, your MAGI is likely above the phaseout cap (~$90,000 for single/HOH in 2023), so the student loan interest deduction would be reduced or fully phased out even if some interest was paid in 2023.
- Also, if the $4,400 refers to interest paid in earlier years (2016–2020) it is not deductible on your 2023 return. Please confirm the year(s) the interest was paid.

4) Treatment of the home office
- Because you have $12,500 in IT contracting (self-employment) income, you can claim a home office deduction for that self-employment activity if the space is used exclusively and regularly for business and is your principal place of business.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5 per sq. ft. up to 300 sq ft → 165 sq ft × $5 = $825. Simple, less recordkeeping.
  b) Regular (actual expense) method: allocate a percentage of home expenses to the office. Your office is 165/780 ≈ 21.15% of the home. Example: rent $1,720 × 12 = $20,640; 21.15% × $20,640 ≈ $4,367 rent portion. You could also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.
- Important limits and considerations:
  - The home office deduction for self-employment cannot create a loss from the business for regular tax purposes; it’s limited to net business income (your Schedule C profit). If your net self-employment income (after business expenses) is less than the home-office allocation, the deduction may be limited and any disallowed portion may be carryforward under certain circumstances (for Form 8829 regular method).
  - For simplicity and given your relatively small self-employment income ($12.5k), the simplified method ($825) is often advisable unless you have other large home-related expenses and want to do the math and keep receipts.
  - If you are an employee (W-2) doing work-from-home for your employer, the home-office deduction is not allowed for employees under current law (2018–2025 suspension).

5) Other business deductions
- If the $950 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses for your contracting work (not employee unreimbursed expenses), you can deduct them on Schedule C (they reduce self-employment net income).
- If they are truly unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job, they are not deductible (suspended for tax years 2018–2025).

6) Self-employment tax and other adjustments
- Your contracting income is subject to self-employment tax (Social Security + Medicare), and you can deduct one-half of your self-employment tax as an adjustment to income.
- You may also be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (20% of qualified business income from a sole proprietorship) which can reduce taxable income; with around $10–12k net business income, QBI could be helpful (approx. up to ~20% of net business income, subject to rules).

7) Forms you will likely need
- Form 1040 (individual return).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report your $12,500 contracting income and related business expenses and to claim home office deduction on Schedule C (simplified) or via Form 8829 for the regular method.
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — to compute self-employment tax and the deduction for half of that tax.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the regular (actual expense) method for the home office.
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you choose to itemize (but likely you will not).
- Form 8995 or 8995-A — for Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (if you qualify; 8995 is the simpler form).
- Schedule 1 — for reporting adjustments to income such as student loan interest (if eligible).
- 1099s / 1098s / 1099-INT / 1099-DIV — report any amounts shown on these as appropriate (interest, dividends, nonemployee compensation, mortgage interest, etc.).

8) Records to keep
- Receipts for charitable donations.
- Documentation of home office exclusive use and square footage (sketch or floor plan, lease statements showing rent paid, utility bills).
- Receipts/invoices for business expenses (the $950 if business-related).
