Thanks — I ran the numbers and put together what you can likely deduct, which filing route will probably be best, how to treat the home office, and which forms you’ll need. I made a couple of assumptions below (I call them out) — please confirm so I can refine anything.

Quick snapshot of income (based on what you gave)
- W-2 wages (TechStart): $84,900
- Self‑employment (IT contracting): $12,500
- Interest: $1,450 (Goldman Sachs Marcus) + $480 (E*TRADE) = $1,930
- Rough total income before adjustments: $99,330

Key points, deductible items, and likely result

1) Self‑employment reporting and taxes
- You should report the contracting income on Schedule C (Form 1040). Any ordinary and necessary business expenses related to that work are deductible on Schedule C.
- If the $950 you listed are business expenses for the contracting work, they reduce your Schedule C net income. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses (i.e., you were an employee and your employer didn’t reimburse those costs), those are NOT deductible for tax years 2018–2025.
- You will owe self‑employment tax on net Schedule C earnings (computed on Schedule SE). You can deduct one‑half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).

2) Home office deduction (you said 165 sq ft exclusive; home = 780 sq ft; rent $1,720/mo)
You can take the home office deduction because you’re self‑employed and the space is used exclusively for the business. Two methods:

- Simplified method
  - $5 × 165 sq ft = $825 (flat deduction).
  - Very simple recordkeeping; no Form 8829 required.

- Regular (actual expenses) method
  - Business use % = 165 / 780 ≈ 21.15%
  - Annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640
  - Business share of rent = $20,640 × 21.15% ≈ $4,369
  - You may also deduct the business share of utilities, renters insurance, etc. (direct expenses for the office are fully deductible).
  - You must use Form 8829 to compute and report the deduction (if using the regular method).
  - The regular method may produce a much larger deduction here (just rent portion ≈ $4,369 vs. simplified $825). That will reduce your Schedule C net income and SE tax.

Important: home office deduction cannot exceed your net income from that business (after other business expenses). Also keep good records: lease, rent paid, utility bills, and show the exclusive business area.

3) Student loan interest ($4,400)
- The student loan interest deduction is capped at $2,500 (an above‑the‑line adjustment) and phases out based on MAGI.
- For 2023 the phaseout for single/head of household is $75,000–$90,000 MAGI. If your MAGI is at or above $90,000 you cannot take the deduction.
- Rough estimate (assuming $950 is business expense and NOT yet deducting home office):
  - Net SE income estimate: 12,500 − 950 = 11,550
  - Estimate SE tax ≈ $1,631 → half SE deduction ≈ $816
  - AGI before student loan deduction ≈ 84,900 + 11,550 + 1,930 − 816 ≈ $97,565
  - That’s above the $90,000 threshold, so you likely cannot take the student loan interest deduction.
- If you use the regular home‑office method and deduct ~ $4,369 rent share on Schedule C, your AGI estimate still likely stays above $90k (rough new AGI ≈ $93,500 in my estimate), so still likely phased out.
- Also note: only interest paid in the tax year (2023) is deductible. Do you mean $4,400 interest paid in 2023, or total interest accrued in prior years? If you didn’t pay $4,400 in 2023, you can’t deduct interest you didn’t pay that year.

4) Charitable contributions ($800)
- Charitable gifts are deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). For 2023 the standard deduction for Head of Household is $20,800.
- Your likely total itemizable deductions (just $800 charity, no mortgage interest or large state/property taxes mentioned) will be far less than the standard deduction, so taking the standard deduction will almost certainly be better.

5) Itemize vs. standard
- Standard deduction (Head of Household, 2023): $20,800.
- Your likely itemized deductions (as reported) look small: $800 charitable + maybe some state taxes (if any) — unlikely to exceed $20,800.
- Recommendation: take the standard deduction unless you have additional significant itemizable items (mortgage interest, large property taxes, medical expenses above threshold, major charitable gifts beyond the $800, etc.).

6) Forms you will likely need
- Form W-2 (TechStart)
- 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC (if your contracting clients issued 1099s) and/or records of payments you received (if clients didn’t issue 1099s)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report contracting income and business expenses
- Schedule SE — to compute self-employment tax
- Form 8829 — if you use the regular method for the home office (not required for simplified)
- Form 1040 (with Schedule 1 attachments for adjustments such as half SE tax deduction)
- 1099-INTs (for your interest income)
- Form 1098-E (if your loan servicer issued it) — documentation of student loan interest paid
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (probably not)
- Arizona state return (AZ Form 140) and any state/local forms

Other practical notes
- If your $950 are employee unreimbursed expenses (not self‑employment expenses) they are NOT deductible under current law — confirm what those $950 are for.
- If you expect similar self‑employment income in future, consider making estimated tax payments (Form 1040-ES) to avoid underpayment penalties. With $11k net contracting income you will have some additional tax but your W-2 withholding may cover a portion — check withholding.
- Keep receipts for charitable donations, rent statements, utility bills, and business expense receipts. Keep floor plan / square footage documentation for the home office.

What I need from you to finalize / refine
1. Is the $4,400 the amount of student loan interest you actually paid in 2023? (If not, what was paid in 2023?)
2. Are the $950 unreimbursed costs business expenses for your contracting work (supplies, travel, software, etc.)? Or are they employee expenses from a W-2 job?
3. Do you have any mortgage interest, property tax, large state tax payments, or other deductible expenses not listed?
4. Do you want me to run estimated tax / take‑home pay calculations showing the tax impact of choosing simplified vs. regular home‑office method?

If you confirm those points I’ll recompute precise estimates (AGI, taxable income, estimated federal tax + SE tax) and list exactly which lines/forms to complete.

