Below is a concise, practical summary of what you can likely do for your 2023 return, what forms you’ll need, and a short list of follow-up questions so you (or your tax preparer) can finalize numbers.

Quick conclusions
- Standard deduction (Head of Household) for 2023 = $20,800. With only the $800 charitable donation as an itemized deduction, you will almost certainly take the standard deduction.
- Home office (as a self‑employed IT contractor) is deductible. Using the regular method (business % of rent/utilities) will almost certainly yield a larger deduction than the simplified method for a 165 sq ft office in a 780 sq ft home.
- Student loan interest: you probably cannot claim the student loan interest deduction for 2023 if your 2023 MAGI is above the phaseout ($75,000–$90,000 for single/HoH). With your reported income it looks likely you exceed $90k unless business adjustments reduce MAGI below the threshold — we’ll need exact 2023 interest paid and certain adjustments to be sure.
- Interest income totaling $1,930 (Goldman + E*TRADE) is reportable; because total taxable interest > $1,500 you’ll need Schedule B.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job are not deductible (suspended by TCJA). If your $950 in “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses related to your contracting work, they are deductible on Schedule C.

What you’ll generally need to file (federal)
- Form 1040
- Schedule 1 (if you have adjustments like half of self‑employment tax, etc.)
- Schedule B — if total interest and ordinary dividends > $1,500 (your interest > $1,500)
- Schedule C — to report the $12,500 contracting income and related business expenses
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax (if net self‑employment earnings ≥ $400)
- Form 8829 — to calculate the regular-method home office deduction (if you choose regular method). The result flows to Schedule C.
- Form 1098‑E — you should receive this from your loan servicer for interest paid in 2023 (used to support any student-loan interest deduction claim)
- Any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC you received from clients — report even if you didn’t receive them
- State return (Arizona) — separate rules may apply there

How the home office works (two methods)
- Simplified: $5 × square feet (max 300 sq ft). For 165 sq ft → $825 deduction.
- Regular (actual expenses): business percentage = 165 / 780 ≈ 21.15%. Apply that percentage to allowable home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs) to determine the deductible portion. Example just using rent: annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640; business portion ≈ $4,369. You can add business share of utilities, internet (business portion), etc. The regular method here is likely larger than $825.
- Requirement: exclusive and regular use for business and principal place of business test. You stated 165 sq ft exclusive — that meets the condition.

Student loan interest specifics
- Deduction limit is up to $2,500 of interest paid in the tax year, but phases out for MAGI between $75k and $90k (single/HoH). If your 2023 MAGI is above the phaseout range, you cannot claim it.
- You wrote $4,400 — confirm whether that is interest paid in 2023 or an aggregate for multiple years. Only interest paid in 2023 matters. Look for Form 1098‑E from Navient showing interest paid in 2023.

Self‑employment notes
- Report the $12,500 on Schedule C; deduct business expenses (home office share, any supplies, software, mileage if applicable, etc.).
- Net self‑employment income is subject to self‑employment tax (Schedule SE). You may deduct half of self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040.
- Keep receipts. If you expect to owe more tax, you may need estimated tax payments.

Charitable donations
- $800 cash donation is deductible only if you itemize. Since standard deduction ($20,800) is likely larger, you would not get any additional tax benefit from the $800 unless you have other itemizable amounts.

Unreimbursed expenses
- If these relate to your W‑2 employment: not deductible for 2018–2025.
- If these relate to your contracting business: deductible on Schedule C (list them as business expenses).

Items that require or are helpful to find/confirm
- Exact amount of student loan interest paid in 2023 (Form 1098‑E).
- Whether the $950 “unreimbursed” expenses are related to contracting (self‑employment) or to your W‑2 job.
- Any other contractor/business expenses (software subscriptions, equipment, travel, phone/internet %, home utilities, supplies) and receipts.
- Did your contracting clients issue 1099‑NEC? If not, you still must report the income.
- Any state/local taxes paid or property tax (if you paid property tax or state income tax and want to itemize SALT — note SALT cap of $10k).
- Precise breakdown of rent/utilities/renter’s insurance/repairs for the home if you want a precise home office calculation.

Example rough calculation (illustrative only)
- Gross income estimate: 84,900 (W‑2) + 12,500 (contract) + 1,930 (interest) = 99,330.
- If you take regular home office (rent portion ≈ $4,369) and no other business expenses: net contract income ≈ 12,500 − 4,369 = 8,131.
- SE tax is computed on ~92.35% of net SE income → self‑employment tax ≈ $1,149; half (~$575) is an adjustment to income.
- After that adjustment AGI likely remains above $90k, so student loan interest deduction would be unavailable unless you have more business expenses/adjustments that lower MAGI below the limit.
(If you want, I can run a precise worksheet with the exact numbers you provide.)

Recommended next steps
1. Confirm the amount of student loan interest paid in 2023 (and get Form 1098‑E).
2. Confirm whether the $950 unreimbursed is for contracting or W‑2 work, and list any other business expenses.
3. Provide rent + utilities + internet + renter’s insurance totals for 2023 if you want a precise home office deduction calculation.
4. Tell me if you want me to calculate estimated tax owed, make a worksheet estimating federal tax, or draft a note to an accountant with these items (I already emailed Vince with the details you gave).

If you want, I can:
- Run a precise calculation/worksheet if you give the numbers above (1098‑E interest paid in 2023; other business expenses; utilities amounts).
- Draft a short email to an accountant with the facts and questions (I can send it if you tell me the recipient).
- Prepare a checklist of documents to hand to a preparer.

Which of the follow-ups above would you like me to do now?

