Quick summary / likely outcome
- Standard deduction for Head of Household (2023): $20,800.  
- Based on the items you listed (charity $800, no mortgage interest shown, limited other deductible items listed), you will most likely take the standard deduction unless you have significant state income/mortgage taxes or other large deductible expenses not listed.  
- Home-office deduction: as a self-employed contractor you can take a home-office deduction that reduces your Schedule C net income — this is separate from deciding to itemize. Using the regular method (allocating rent by % of home used for business) looks materially larger than the simplified method for your numbers, so regular is probably better.  
- Student loan interest deduction: unlikely available at full value. The deduction phases out based on MAGI (for 2023 the phaseout for single/HOH is roughly $75k–$90k). Your combined income (W‑2 + contracting + interest) likely pushes MAGI above the phaseout, so you probably cannot claim the student loan interest deduction unless your 2023 MAGI ends up under the threshold after business adjustments.  
- Self-employment tax / estimated payments: you’ll owe self-employment tax on net contracting income and should confirm whether estimated taxes are/were needed.

Detail and calculations (estimates)
- Income you reported (gross): W-2 $84,900 + contracting $12,500 + interest $1,450 + interest $480 = $99,330 total gross income.  
- Home office (two methods):
  - Simplified method: 165 sq ft × $5 = $825 deduction.
  - Regular method (allocation): 165 / 780 = 21.15% business use. Rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640 × 21.15% ≈ $4,368. (If you have utilities, renters’ insurance, repairs, etc., their business portion can be added and increase the deduction.) Regular method here yields much more than simplified ($4,368 vs $825), so regular is likely better.
- Effect on taxable income: the home-office deduction is reported on Schedule C (via Form 8829 if you use the regular method) and reduces your self‑employment net income, which reduces both income tax and self‑employment tax.
- Student loan interest ($4,400): the deduction is limited to interest you actually paid in 2023 and phased out above MAGI thresholds. With your likely MAGI (~$99k before adjustments), the deduction phases out to zero for 2023. If you actually paid student interest in 2023 and your MAGI after Schedule C adjustments falls under the threshold, you could get a partial deduction — but based on numbers given it’s unlikely.
- Unreimbursed expenses $950: if these are business expenses related to your IT contracting (supplies, software, mileage, etc.), they are deductible on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed expenses relating to a W‑2 job, they are no longer deductible for 2018–2025 under current law.
- Interest from Marcus + E*TRADE = $1,450 + $480 = $1,930. Because total interest > $1,500 you will likely need to file Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends).
- QBI (Qualified Business Income) deduction: you may be eligible for a 20% QBI deduction on qualified business income from the contracting work (claimed on Form 1040) — the benefit depends on taxable income and type of business. Given the small size of the contracting income, any QBI deduction would be modest but worth checking.

Forms you will likely need (2023)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return).
- Schedule 1 (additional income/adjustments) — if applicable for adjustments.
- Schedule B — if your taxable interest > $1,500 (likely required here).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report IT contracting income and business expenses.
- Schedule SE — to compute self-employment tax.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the regular method for home office. (If you use simplified method, you do not use Form 8829.)
- 1099-NEC or 1099-MISC (you should collect any 1099s from clients; you still report income even if you did not receive 1099s).
- 1099-INT statements for Marcus and E*TRADE interest.
- 1098-E (student loan interest statement), if you received one for interest paid in 2023 — but remember deduction is limited by MAGI and only for interest paid in 2023.
- Schedule A only if you choose to itemize (unlikely given current numbers unless you have significant additional deductible items).
- Form 1040-ES — for making estimated tax payments if you expect to owe self-employment tax or income tax not covered by withholding.

Practical recommendations / next steps
1. Gather documents:
   - W-2 from TechStart.
   - 1099-NEC / records showing $12,500 contracting income and any receipts for business expenses (the $950 and any other costs).
