Agent Response: Thanks — good detail. Below is a clear, practical breakdown of what’s likely deductible, whether you should itemize, how to treat the home office, and which tax forms you’ll need. I’ll flag places where I need one or two clarifying facts to be precise.

Quick summary
- Likely best to take the 2023 Head‑of‑Household standard deduction ($20,800) unless you have other large deductible items (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes, big medical expenses). Based on the items you listed, itemizing probably won’t beat the standard deduction.
- Home office: you can claim a home‑office deduction only for the self‑employment/contracting income ($12,500). You cannot deduct a home office for W‑2 work (except extremely rare employer situations).
- Student loan interest: only interest paid in the tax year (2023) is potentially deductible (up to $2,500, subject to income phaseouts). Interest paid in prior years (2016–2020) is not deductible on your 2023 return.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses for W‑2 work are not deductible for 2018–2025 under current law (generally not deductible).
- You’ll need Form 1040 plus Schedule C (for contracting), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), and either Form 8829 (regular home office method) or use the simplified method (no 8829). You may also use Form 8995 (QBI) to claim the 20% qualified business income deduction for your contracting income.

Details and calculations (based on the numbers you gave)
1) Standard deduction vs. itemizing
- Head of Household standard deduction for 2023 = $20,800.
- Itemizable items you listed: charitable cash $800. You did not list mortgage interest, property taxes, or SALT totaling near the standard deduction. So with only $800 charity and no mortgage interest, itemizing will almost certainly be lower than the standard deduction.
- Note: student loan interest (if paid in 2023) is an “above‑the‑line” adjustment to income (Schedule 1) and doesn’t affect the itemize vs standard choice; it reduces AGI whether or not you itemize.

2) Student loan interest
- Only interest paid in 2023 is eligible for the student loan interest deduction (up to $2,500), subject to MAGI phaseouts.
- If your $4,400 figure covers years 2016–2020 (and not 2023), those are not deductible on your 2023 return.
- If you did pay interest in 2023, you’ll need Form 1098‑E from the loan servicer (shows interest paid). If you want, tell me how much interest you actually paid in 2023 and I’ll estimate whether you’re within the phaseout range.

3) Home office deduction — eligibility & methods
- Qualifies only for your self‑employment activity (the $12,500 IT contracting). Requirements: exclusive and regular use of that 165 sq ft space as your principal place of business (you said exclusive — good).
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft. For 165 sq ft → 165 × $5 = $825 deduction. Simple, no Form 8829.
  b) Regular method: allocate a share of actual home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.) based on square footage. Your numbers:
     - Annual rent = $1,720 × 12 = $20,640.
     - Home office percentage = 165 / 780 ≈ 21.15%.
     - Home office share of rent alone ≈ $20,640 × 21.15% ≈ $4,368 (plus allocated share of utilities, internet, renters insurance, etc., if you want to include them).
- Which to pick? Simplified is easier and often better for smaller offices or when you don’t want to track shared expense allocations. The regular method may give a larger deduction here (rent share alone > $4k) but remember:
  - Home office deduction cannot exceed net profit from the business (you cannot create or increase a loss from the deduction — excess may carry forward in some cases).
  - The deduction applies only to your self‑employment income; it will not reduce W‑2 wages.
- You’ll report the home office deduction on Schedule C and, for the regular method, compute details on Form 8829. For simplified, include it directly on Schedule C (no 8829).

4) Self‑employment tax, deductions, and QBI
- Your $12,500 contracting income is subject to self‑employment tax (Schedule SE) on net earnings (approx 92.35% of net profit), plus income tax.
- Half of the self‑employment tax is an above‑the‑line deduction on Schedule 1.
- You may be eligible for a Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Section 199A) of up to 20% of qualified business income from the Schedule C profit. With your income level and business type, you’ll likely be eligible; use Form 8995 to claim the QBI deduction (or 8995‑A if complex).

5) Other income you listed
- Interest: $1,450 (Goldman Sachs Marcus) and $480 (E*TRADE) — report these on Form 1040 as interest (you should receive 1099‑INTs).
- If E*TRADE also includes brokerage transactions, you may receive 1099‑B for sales (capital gains/losses). Report accordingly.

6) Unreimbursed expenses ($950)
- Unreimbursed employee expenses for W‑2 employees are generally not deductible for 2018–2025. If the $950 are related to your self‑employment (ordinary and necessary business expenses), they are deductible on Schedule C. If they are truly unreimbursed expenses for W‑2 job, they are not deductible.

7) Forms you will likely need
- Form 1040.
- Schedule 1 (adjustments to income: e.g., student loan interest if applicable).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report $12,500 and business expenses, including home office.
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax).
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you use the regular method.
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) for QBI deduction.
- Schedule A only if you itemize (probably you won’t).
- 1099‑NEC or 1099‑MISC (if you received any for contracting), W‑2 from TechStart, 1099‑INTs, 1099‑B (if applicable).

Practical next steps / what I need from you to be more precise
1. Confirm whether the $4,400 student loan interest is interest you paid in 2023 or earlier years (2016–2020). If you have a 2023 Form 1098‑E amount, give me that number.
2. Do you have any mortgage interest, property tax, or other significant deductible expenses not listed (medical > 7.5% AGI, large SALT, etc.)?
3. Do you want me to run sample calculations comparing:
   - Simplified home office vs regular home office, and
   - Itemizing vs the standard deduction,
   using the numbers you provided and reasonable assumptions (I can show estimated taxable income, self‑employment tax, and tax savings)?
4. Do you have 1099 forms for the contracting income or interest (if you want me to list exact documents to gather)?

If you want, I can run the sample calculation now showing:
- net self‑employment profit after home office (both methods),
- estimated self‑employment tax and income tax impact,
- whether itemizing would ever make sense given your numbers.

Would you like me to do that? If yes, please confirm whether the $4,400 student loan interest was paid in 2023 or earlier.
